# Protocol & SDKs

WebSocket-only application protocol (ADR-0001). Node/TS and Go SDKs first (ADR-0022). URLs never contain tokens.

## Frames

- Envelope: `{ id, op, token? , idempotencyKey?, ...payload }`. Reply: `{ id, ok, data?, error? }`.
- Auth is the first frame: `{"op":"auth","token":"..."}`. Credentials in the frame, never the URL.
- Every state-changing app op carries an `idempotencyKey`; repeating it returns the original result without re-applying.
- App ops: `schedule.set`, `schedule.cancel`, `schedule.extend`, `schedule.throttle`, `delivery.ack`, `workflow.start`, `signal`.
- Admin ops: `app.create`, `app.token.rotate`, `app.token.revoke`, `workflow.publish`, `calendar.set`, `calendar.next`, `calendar.occurrences`, `start-schedule.set`, `dashboard.stats`, `dashboard.list`, `delivery.replay`, `delivery.cancel`.
- Delivery push: `{ op:"delivery", deliveryId, scheduleId, event, data }`.

## SDK responsibilities (own these, don't leak to callers)

- Authentication, reconnection with backoff, request/reply correlation by `id`, delivery acknowledgement, idempotency-key handling.
- Stable delivery ID so handlers stay idempotent by delivery ID or business object.
- `ack` means receipt only. Business completion is a separate correlated `signal`.

## Rules

- Keep the SDK surface small — one method per protocol op. No speculative config.
- Node SDK is zero-dependency beyond `ws`; Go SDK uses `coder/websocket`.
- Never log tokens.
- Protocol changes: update both SDKs and the README op list together.
